---
/**
 * Portable Text, rendered. The one place the serializer map lives.
 *
 * ── IT IS TRANSPARENT, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN ──────────────────────────
 *
 * This emits a FLAT SEQUENCE OF BLOCKS and no container. Two reasons, and both
 * would break if it wrapped:
 *
 *   - The vertical rhythm ramp in base.css is `article.detail > * + *` — DIRECT
 *     children. A wrapper here would put every block one level too deep and the
 *     entire ramp would stop matching, silently, leaving uniform default spacing
 *     that reads as a design choice rather than a bug.
 *   - `article.detail` also holds page-level siblings that are not Portable Text
 *     at all — the podcast embed, the topics nav. They need the same ramp, so the
 *     container belongs to the page, which is the only thing that knows about
 *     them.
 *
 * Verified rather than assumed: `<PortableText>` renders `nodes.map(...)` with
 * nothing around it, so its blocks land as children of whatever contains this.
 *
 * ── WHY THERE IS ALMOST NO MAP ───────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * astro-portabletext's defaults already emit the HTML this design wants:
 * `normal` -> <p>, h1-h6 -> the matching heading, `blockquote` -> <blockquote>,
 * `bullet` -> <ul>, `number` -> <ol>, list items -> <li>, `em` -> <em>,
 * `strong` -> <strong>, `link` -> <a href>. base.css styles all of those as
 * element roles, so there is nothing to override.
 *
 * So the map carries the CUSTOM TYPES the schema adds that have no HTML
 * equivalent for a library to guess at — `figure` and `code` — plus the ONE
 * default whose element is semantically wrong for this content: `strike-through`,
 * which the library renders as `<del>` and which is `<s>` here. See Strike.astro
 * for why those are not interchangeable.
 *
 * Resist adding an override that restates a default; the default is the same
 * markup with less to keep in step. `strike-through` earns its place because it
 * changes the element, not the styling.
 *
 * `h2` earns its place a third way: same element, same styling, plus an `id` the
 * library cannot know is wanted. The rail's "On This Page" links target those ids,
 * and the id function is shared with the page so the two cannot disagree. See
 * Heading.astro and headingId.ts.
 *
 * ── ONE TYPE LEFT UNBUILT ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * `table`, which the `article` schema allows and which NO published document
 * uses. It is not built on spec: a serializer written against no content is a
 * guess at what the content would have looked like. `onMissingComponent` stays at
 * its default, which prints a warning naming the type at build time, so the first
 * document to use one announces itself instead of rendering nothing.
 *
 * The other two that were listed here are done. `image` was folded into `figure`
 * and removed from the schema; `pre` was renamed to `code` — schema and data both
 * — and is built. Neither is coming back.
 *
 * ── TWO THINGS THE DEFAULTS GET RIGHT THAT ARE WORTH KNOWING ─────────────────
 *
 *   - `marks` on a span is a MIXED array: decorator names (`em`, `strong`) and
 *     `_key`s pointing into the block's own `markDefs` (`af2d9c8a74bd`). The
 *     library resolves the two through `buildMarksTree` and hands a `link` mark
 *     its resolved `markDef`, which is the part of a hand-written serializer most
 *     likely to be wrong.
 *   - Nested lists are handled by `nestLists` in `html` mode, so a deeper list
 *     becomes a child of the last <li> — the nesting HTML actually allows. The
 *     first real article is single-level, so this is untested against content.
 *
 * ── THE `underline` PROBLEM WAS FIXED UPSTREAM, NOT HERE ────────────────────
 *
 * The library renders `underline` as `<span style="text-decoration: underline">`,
 * and an inline `text-decoration` collides with the underline that carries link
 * affordance in base.css — an underlined word would be indistinguishable from a
 * link. Two published articles had one.
 *
 * There is deliberately NO override for it. The decorator was removed from every
 * Portable Text field in the schema instead (studio-next/schemas/portableText.ts),
 * on the rule that THE UNDERLINE SHOULD ONLY EVER MEAN "LINK", and the two
 * articles were corrected. Styling it here would have been the wrong fix: it would
 * make a mark legitimate that the design has no room for, and every future
 * document could then carry one.
 *
 * If an `underline` ever reaches this renderer it means content predates that
 * schema change — Sanity keeps marks it no longer offers — and the fix is the
 * document, not this file.
 */
import {PortableText} from 'astro-portabletext'
/* From `astro-portabletext/types`, NOT from `@portabletext/types` — which is where
   it originates but is only a TRANSITIVE dependency here. pnpm does not hoist, so
   importing it directly fails to resolve, and adding it to package.json would
   declare a dependency this project does not actually have. The library re-exports
   it for precisely this case. */
import type {TypedObject} from 'astro-portabletext/types'
import Figure from './Figure.astro'
import Code from './Code.astro'
import Heading from './Heading.astro'
import Strike from './Strike.astro'

interface Props {
  /** A Portable Text array. Null and empty both render nothing, which is what a
      caseStudy — with no `bodyText` field at all — needs. */
  value?: TypedObject[] | null
}

const {value} = Astro.props
---

{
  value && value.length > 0 && (
    <PortableText
      value={value}
      components={{
        /* Keyed by the stored `_type`, which now matches the generated union —
           this member was named `pre` until 2026-08-26 and both the schema and the
           25 existing blocks were corrected. See Code.astro. */
        type: {figure: Figure, code: Code},
        /* Keyed by block STYLE, where `type` above is keyed by `_type`. */
        block: {h2: Heading},
        mark: {'strike-through': Strike},
      }}
    />
  )
}
